WEBVTT

00:00:01.160 --> 00:00:05.330
Here is a really pretty cable that can be
used to easily add interest

00:00:05.363 --> 00:00:08.170
and texture to your next project.

00:00:08.203 --> 00:00:14.650
It's 2 four stitch cables side by side,
surrounded by purl stitches.

00:00:14.683 --> 00:00:17.930
This is a classic Arrows Out cable.

00:00:17.963 --> 00:00:22.183
The multiple is 14 stitches by six rows.

00:00:22.417 --> 00:00:25.370
And the chart looks like this.

00:00:25.403 --> 00:00:27.530
This chart is written
for machine knitters.

00:00:27.563 --> 00:00:30.490
It's shown from the purl side.

00:00:30.523 --> 00:00:35.920
This symbol reflects knit stitches,
and this symbol reflects the manually

00:00:35.953 --> 00:00:41.210
formed purl stitches that add
to the texture of this stitch pattern.

00:00:41.243 --> 00:00:45.280
These symbols indicate the stitches
that will be crossed and the direction

00:00:45.313 --> 00:00:49.320
of the transfers to form the cables.

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:52.730
To create this cable,
it's a two-step process.

00:00:52.763 --> 00:00:54.570
Give it a try.

00:00:54.603 --> 00:00:59.760
Cast on 40 stitches and knit a few
rows and add a little bit of weight.

00:01:00.040 --> 00:01:01.880
Mark your needle bed as shown.

00:01:01.913 --> 00:01:03.440
I like to just use a pencil.

00:01:03.473 --> 00:01:05.410
It's easily erased.

00:01:05.443 --> 00:01:09.690
You'll be crossing needles 2 through 5
on the left and needles

00:01:09.723 --> 00:01:11.970
2 through 5 on the right.

00:01:12.003 --> 00:01:18.650
Reading the cable symbols,
the broken lines are transferred first

00:01:18.683 --> 00:01:21.690
and the solid lines are transferred last.

00:01:21.723 --> 00:01:23.250
Think of it this way.

00:01:23.283 --> 00:01:28.170
The first stitch is transferred will
appear on the public side of the knitting.

00:01:28.203 --> 00:01:34.320
In this example, they'll be pointing
out away from the center of the design.

00:01:34.800 --> 00:01:40.360
The second set of stitches transferred
will be behind those stitches.

00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:44.240
Start by transferring
the first set of needles.

00:01:44.440 --> 00:01:48.930
Put the stitches from needles four
and five on a two-prong tool,

00:01:48.963 --> 00:01:53.480
turn the tool to get it
and the stitches out of the way.

00:01:53.600 --> 00:01:58.720
Put the stitches from needle number two
and three on a two-prong tool and transfer

00:01:58.753 --> 00:02:02.480
them left to fill the emptied needles.

00:02:03.120 --> 00:02:08.560
Finish the cable by replacing the held
stitches to the newly emptied needles.

00:02:09.320 --> 00:02:14.890
Repeat this on the right side of zero,
but we'll do it the opposite way.

00:02:14.923 --> 00:02:20.370
Put the stitches on needles four and five
on the tool, turn the tool to make room,

00:02:20.403 --> 00:02:24.210
transfer stitches number two and three
to the emptied needles,

00:02:24.243 --> 00:02:29.440
and then transfer the held stitches
to the newly emptied needles.

00:02:30.920 --> 00:02:33.890
It's time to knit the row
and complete the cable.

00:02:33.923 --> 00:02:37.920
But first,
you may be tempted to pull out all

00:02:37.953 --> 00:02:40.610
the needles to make
the knitting a little easier.

00:02:40.643 --> 00:02:44.490
In this simple stitch pattern,
this may work, but it is suggested

00:02:44.523 --> 00:02:47.010
that you avoid doing this.

00:02:47.043 --> 00:02:50.810
If you're transferring more than just
a few needles like we are here,

00:02:50.843 --> 00:02:53.490
it can distort the position
of the needles,

00:02:53.523 --> 00:02:57.720
and pulling them all the way out could
distort it even more, making it even

00:02:57.753 --> 00:03:00.730
more difficult to knit, not easier.

00:03:00.763 --> 00:03:05.690
So keep those needles in middle position.

00:03:05.723 --> 00:03:08.970
Following the pattern, knit six rows.

00:03:09.003 --> 00:03:12.280
Now, step two of this process is to add
the column of purl

00:03:12.313 --> 00:03:15.320
stitches next to the cables.

00:03:15.960 --> 00:03:20.160
Working one column of the stitches
at a time, drop the stitch nearest

00:03:20.193 --> 00:03:23.410
to the cable or needle number 6.

00:03:23.443 --> 00:03:29.200
Position your latch tool at the bottom
of the column and drop the stitch down.

00:03:30.680 --> 00:03:34.520
Latch up each stitch,
forming purl stitches on the side

00:03:34.553 --> 00:03:36.450
that is away from you.

00:03:36.483 --> 00:03:40.120
Then carefully rehang
that stitch on the needle.

00:03:41.000 --> 00:03:46.440
Repeat this on needle number 7,
manually latching up a column of stitches.

00:03:48.280 --> 00:03:52.840
Next, create columns of purl stitches
between the two cables on needles

00:03:52.873 --> 00:03:56.200
left one and right one.

00:04:11.680 --> 00:04:16.360
Finish by latching up stitches

00:04:16.393 --> 00:04:19.880
number 6 and 7 on the right.

00:04:21.917 --> 00:04:25.600
Following the pattern,
it's time to cross the cables again.

00:04:26.760 --> 00:04:31.290
Now, if you're like me, you may not
want to wait to see your progress.

00:04:31.323 --> 00:04:34.760
The first time I knit cables
on the machine, I crawed under

00:04:34.793 --> 00:04:37.447
the table to see the results.

00:04:37.480 --> 00:04:40.320
Now, you might consider holding your
camera behind your knitting

00:04:40.353 --> 00:04:41.850
and taking a photo.

00:04:41.883 --> 00:04:44.727
It's easier on the back and knees.

00:04:44.760 --> 00:04:45.600
Happy knitting.

